[Music]  by now you're probably familiar with  mrna vaccines they're the ones most  commonly used in the u.s against covet  but scientists think this technology can  do a lot more like targeted disease you  may have heard of too lyme disease which  is spread by ticks some have called it a  silent epidemic one that's been growing  between 2018 and 2019 alone the number  of cases went up about four percent  let's take a close look at how this new  experimental shot works and why it could  be a game changer if it gets approved  for humans  each year about 30 000 cases of lyme  disease are reported to the cdc but the  actual number may be as high as nearly  half a million to understand what those  numbers mean for patients i called up  brian fallon a lyme disease expert at  columbia university  if you don't catch it detect it early  it can lead to a variety of serious  problems it could lead to  neurological problems such as meningitis  facial palsy chronic cognitive problems  it can lead to psychiatric problems  depression irritability etc brian said  that even with antibiotics some patients  end up with these long-term debilitating  symptoms it's similar to in some ways  the  long covet syndrome we've had long lyme  syndrome for a long time but now it's  widely recognized that microbes can  cause these persistent symptoms and lead  to these persistent symptoms brian isn't  part of the team that's developing the  new vaccine but like other lyme experts  he thinks we need something novel and we  need something new in the fight against  lyme disease and this definitely  offers that promise  mrna isn't the only thing that makes the  vaccine novel most vaccines help the  body attack a pathogen that causes the  disease for covid that stars kovi too an  mrna vaccine like the one against covid  varies orders an mrna molecule into our  cells those mrna instructions are for  making a harmless version of the viruses  spike protein our cells then make that  protein which spurs the immune system to  build up defenses against it and the  virus not so with this new mrna lyme  disease vaccine whose development  preceded the pandemic by years  researchers have been developing mrna  technology for decades errol figgrick  the yellow researcher who was working on  the vaccine told me it targets 19  proteins in deotek saliva instead of the  bacterium that actually causes lyme  disease so this is a new way of  developing vaccines when a deer tick  bites it latches on and feeds for days  errol told me it takes about 36 hours  for borrelia burgdorferi the lyme  disease causing bacteria to make its way  into a person as the tick is feeding if  you can prevent the tick from feeding  early on  you have a good chance of preventing  lyme disease his vaccine which hasn't  been tested in humans does that in two  ways first it creates a kind of alert  system that's important because the tick  bite doesn't itch the way a mosquito  bite would so it often goes undetected  allowing for silent transmission so our  goal is to make it easily recognizable  that recognition signal is redness on  the skin where the tick is feeding the  redness is the earliest hallmark  associated with acquired tick resistance  this redness is different from the rash  that some patients develop when they get  lyme disease it's part of the immune  system's attack on the lyme  disease-causing bacteria guinea pigs  immunized with aryl's three-dose mrna  vaccine and then bitten by deer ticks  two weeks after their last dose  developed this immune-driven redness as  you can see here it appeared about 18  hours after the ticks latched on errol  said that if people react similarly to  the vaccine they'd be more likely to  notice they've been bitten and they  could immediately remove the tick before  the bacteria it was carrying had a  chance to infect them but even if a  person missed that red alert the vaccine  could still help fend up ticks and lyme  disease transmission that's because the  immune response that produces against  tick saliva helps stop the ticks from  feeding just like you eating a meal that  was too hot or a drink that was too hot  ticks that latched on to vaccinated  guinea pigs ate less and they fell off  their host sooner all this amounted to  protection from infection with the  bacteria that causes lyme disease none  of the researchers 16 vaccinated guinea  pigs tested positive for the bacteria  after they were bitten whereas nearly  half of the unvaccinated ones did  [Music]  so far this mrna vaccine has only been  tested in guinea pigs and mice next up  is testing in other animals including  primates the goal is to eventually test  it in humans before covid vaccines  normally took about 10 years from start  to uh clinical development kovit has  shown us that's possible in  a year  really remarkable i don't think that  five years is a unrealistic expectation  maybe sooner maybe a bit later when  errol and his lab started working on  their shot years ago mrna vaccines were  still an unproven technology but kovit  has changed all that we now know that  mrna vaccines are very effective plus  the fact that this vaccine targets the  animal that spreads slime means it could  be helpful in fighting other diseases  spread by deer ticks and even other  ticks that could translate into fewer  jabs and more protection for us  i found this story through someone i  follow on social media if you have ideas  for cool research i should cover please  leave it in the comments and while  you're at it subscribe to my channel  that means hit subscribe hasta la  proxima

